qiuxi.translation:
The silver candle's autumn light is cold on the painted screen;
with a small silk fan she swats at the drifting fireflies.
On the palace steps the night is cool as water;
lying down, she watches the Herd Boy and the Weaving Girl stars.

chunwang.translation:
The country is broken, but the mountains and rivers remain;
spring in the city, and grass and trees grow thick.
Feeling the times, flowers seem to shed tears;
hating the parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires have burned three months on end;
a letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.
I scratch my white head and the hair grows shorter still,
so thin it can hardly bear a hairpin.

shanjuqiuming.translation:
In the empty mountain, after fresh rain,
the evening air has turned to autumn.
The bright moon shines among the pines;
the clear spring flows over the stones.
The bamboos rustle - washerwomen coming home;
the lotus leaves stir - a fishing boat going down.
Let the spring fragrance fade as it will;
the young nobleman is quite content to stay.

dengyueyanglou.translation:
I heard of Lake Dongting long ago;
today at last I climb Yueyang Tower.
Wu and Chu are split apart, south-east and east;
heaven and earth float on it day and night.
From family and friends, not a single word;
old and ill, I have only a lone boat.
War horses stand beyond the passes in the north;
leaning on the rail, my tears and snot flow.

songyouren.translation:
Green hills lie across the north wall;
white water circles the eastern city.
Here, once we part,
you are a lone tumbleweed going ten thousand li.
A drifting cloud - the wanderer's mind;
the setting sun - an old friend's feeling.
We wave our hands and you leave from here;
the parting horses neigh and neigh.

yueyejishe.translation:
The garrison drum has stopped all travel;
on the border in autumn, the cry of one wild goose.
From tonight the dew turns white;
the moon is brighter over my home village.
I have brothers, but all are scattered;
I have no home from which to ask if they live or die.
Letters I send never arrive,
and all the more so as the war has not ended.

linDongting.translation:
In the eighth month the lake water is level with the shore;
it holds the void and merges with the clear sky.
Mist steams over the marsh of Yunmeng;
waves shake the walls of Yueyang.
I want to cross but have no boat or oar;
living idle at home, I am ashamed in an age so enlightened.
Sitting, I watch the men who fish,
and can only feel in vain the wish to have fish.

cibeigushan.translation:
The traveller's road runs out beyond the green hills;
my boat moves on before the green water.
The tide is level and the two banks are wide;
the wind is fair and a single sail hangs full.
The sun rises from the sea out of the last of night;
spring on the river enters the old year.
How can a letter home get through?
The wild geese flying back, to Luoyang's side.

huanghelou.translation:
The man of old has already ridden a yellow crane away;
here only the empty Yellow Crane Tower remains.
The yellow crane once gone does not come back;
white clouds for a thousand years drift idly on.
In the clear river, every tree of Hanyang stands distinct;
sweet grass grows thick on Parrot Island.
As the sun sets, where is my home?
Mist and waves on the river make me sad.

denggao.translation:
The wind is sharp, the sky high, and the gibbons' cries are sad;
on the clear islet, white sand, the birds wheel and turn.
The endless falling leaves come rustling down;
the unending Yangtze rolls in.
Ten thousand li away in autumn's grief, always a guest;
a hundred years of sickness, alone I climb the terrace.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened the frost on my temples;
in ruin, I have just given up the cup of cloudy wine.

dengfenghuangtai.translation:
On Phoenix Terrace the phoenix once came to roam;
The phoenix has gone, the terrace is empty, and the river flows on by itself.
The flowers and grass of the Wu palaces lie buried along hidden paths;
The robes and caps of the Jin court have become ancient grave mounds.
The Three Mountains half fall away beyond the blue sky;
The Two Rivers are split in the middle by Egret Isle.
It is always because floating clouds can block out the sun
That I cannot see Chang'an, and it fills me with sorrow.

jinse.translation:
The splendid zither, for no reason, has fifty strings;
Every string and every bridge calls to mind the years of my youth.
Zhuangzi in a morning dream was lost in a butterfly;
Emperor Wang gave his spring heart to the cuckoo.
On the wide sea the moon is bright, and the pearls have tears;
At Blue Field the sun is warm, and the jade gives off mist.
Could this feeling wait until it became a memory?
Even then, at the time, I was already dazed and lost.

wuti1.translation:
Meeting is hard, and parting is just as hard;
The east wind has no strength, and the hundred flowers are fading.
The spring silkworm spins its silk only when it dies;
The candle's tears dry only when it has burned to ash.
At dawn, before the mirror, I grieve only that my cloud-like hair has changed;
Chanting at night, I must feel the moonlight cold.
From here to Penglai Mountain there is not much road;
Blue Bird, I ask you kindly to go and look for me.

shuxiang.translation:
Where should I look for the Chancellor's shrine?
Outside Brocade Official City, where the cypresses stand dark and dense.
Green grass by the steps shows its spring colour for no one;
Orioles behind the leaves sing sweetly in vain.
Three visits, again and again, to trouble him for a plan for all under Heaven;
He opened the way for two reigns and carried it on, with an old minister's heart.
He died before the army's campaign could win;
It leaves heroes for ever with tears soaking their lapels.

kezhi.translation:
South of my house and north of my house, spring water everywhere;
I see only flocks of gulls coming day after day.
The flowery path has never been swept for a guest;
My thatch gate is opened today for the first time for you.
The market is far, so the dishes have no second flavour;
The house is poor, so the jar holds only old wine.
If you are willing to drink with my old neighbour,
I will call him over the fence and we will finish the last cups.

yuexiadouzhuo.translation:
Among the flowers, a pot of wine;
I drink alone, with no one close.
I raise my cup to invite the bright moon;
With my shadow facing me, we make three.
The moon, for its part, does not know how to drink;
My shadow merely follows my body.
For a while I keep company with the moon and my shadow;
To enjoy myself I must catch the spring.
I sing, and the moon lingers;
I dance, and my shadow tangles and scatters.
While sober, we share our joy together;
After I am drunk, each goes a separate way.
Let us make a lasting friendship free of ordinary feeling,
And agree to meet far away in the Milky Way.

xinglunan1.translation:
Clear wine in golden cups, ten thousand coins a measure;
Rare delicacies on jade plates, worth ten thousand coins.
I stop my cup, throw down my chopsticks, and cannot eat;
I draw my sword, look all around, and my heart is lost.
I want to cross the Yellow River, but ice blocks the stream;
I am about to climb the Taihang Mountains, but snow fills the peaks.
At leisure I might fish on a green brook,
Or suddenly ride a boat and dream of the side of the sun.
The road is hard! The road is hard!
So many forks in it - where am I now?
The time will come when a long wind breaks the waves;
I will hoist my cloud-sail straight up and cross the deep blue sea.

tianmuyin.translation:
Travellers from the sea speak of Yingzhou;
In the vague mist and waves it is truly hard to find.
The people of Yue speak of Tianmu;
Through clouds that flicker in and out it can perhaps be seen.
Tianmu joins the sky and lies across the heavens,
Its force rising above the Five Peaks and covering Chicheng.
Tiantai, forty-eight thousand feet high,
Seems to topple to the south-east before it.
Wanting to follow this, I dream of Wu and Yue,
And in one night I fly across the moon over Mirror Lake.
The lake moon lights my shadow
And sees me on to Shan Stream.
The lodging of Lord Xie is there still today;
Green waters ripple, and clear-voiced gibbons cry.
On my feet are Lord Xie's clogs,
And I climb the ladder up to the blue clouds.
Halfway up the cliff I see the sun rise from the sea;
In the air I hear the cock of Heaven.
A thousand cliffs, ten thousand turns, the path is never fixed;
Lost among flowers, leaning on rocks, suddenly it is dusk.
Bears roar and dragons chant, shaking the rocks and springs;
The deep woods make me tremble, the layered peaks startle me.
Clouds are dark green, about to rain;
Waters are pale and still, giving off mist.
Lightning cracks, thunderbolts split;
Hills and ridges crumble.
The stone doors of the cave-heaven
Swing open from the middle with a crash.
The blue void is vast, its bottom cannot be seen;
Sun and moon shine on the terraces of gold and silver.
Rainbows for robes, winds for horses,
The lords of the clouds come down in crowds.
Tigers play the zither, phoenixes turn the chariots;
The immortals stand in rows like hemp.
Suddenly my soul shudders and my spirit stirs;
I start up in a daze and sigh long.
I am aware only of the pillow and mat under me;
The mist and clouds of a moment ago are gone.
The pleasures of the world are all like this;
Since ancient times, all things have been water flowing east.
I take leave of you and go - when shall I come back?
For now I will let my white deer graze among green cliffs;
When I must travel, I will ride it to visit famous mountains.
How could I bow my brows and bend my waist to serve the powerful and great,
And so be unable to open my face in joy?

chunjianghuayueye.translation:
The spring river's tide runs level with the sea;
Over the sea the bright moon rises with the tide.
Shimmering, it follows the waves for thousands and tens of thousands of li;
Where on the spring river is there no moonlight?
The river winds around the fragrant meadows;
The moon on the flowering woods makes every blossom look like frozen drops.
Frost seems to flow in the air, yet its flying is not felt;
The white sand on the shore cannot be seen.
River and sky are one colour, without a speck of dust;
Bright and clear in the air hangs one lone wheel of the moon.
Who by the river first saw the moon?
In what year did the river moon first shine on people?
Generation after generation of people goes on without end;
The river moon, year after year, looks much the same.
I do not know whom the river moon is waiting for;
I only see the Yangtze sending its waters on.
A single white cloud drifts away, far and long;
On Green Maple Shore the sorrow is more than one can bear.
Whose boatman is out tonight in his small boat?
In what moonlit tower is someone longing?
Pity the moon lingering above the tower;
It should be shining on the dressing table of the one who is apart.
It cannot be rolled away with the curtains of the jade window;
On the washing stone it is brushed off and comes back again.
At this hour we look at each other but cannot hear;
I wish I could follow the moonlight and flow on to shine on you.
Wild geese fly far, but the light does not pass with them;
Fish and dragons dive and leap, and the water makes ripples.
Last night I dreamed by a still pool of falling flowers;
Pity that spring is half over and I am still not home.
The river water carries spring away, and it is almost gone;
The moon over the river pool slants once more to the west.
The slanting moon sinks, hidden in the sea mist;
From Jieshi to Xiaoxiang the road has no end.
I do not know how many ride the moonlight home;
The setting moon stirs feeling that spills over the trees by the river.
